<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thermae Himeraeae</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Termini Imerese</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thermae Himeraeae, modern Termini Imerese, was a town on the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a>, founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> in 407 BCE near hot springs, with survivors of Himera, which they had destroyed two years earlier. It later became a Roman town and was the birthplace of Agathocles, tyrant of Syracuse.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thermae-himeraeae.jpg" alt="Thermae Himeraeae" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Clemensfranz · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Termini_Imerese_Stadthaus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The site has been populated since prehistoric times. Its documented history begins after the second Battle of Himera in 409 BCE, when the older neighbouring town of Himera was completely destroyed by the Carthaginian army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal-mago">Hannibal Mago</a>. Survivors moved to a site then called Thermae, today Termini, which became Himera's successor. The new town took its name from the hot springs for which it was known, whose discovery legend connected with the wanderings of Hercules. It quickly became a considerable town, though it stayed under Carthaginian rule.</p><p>In 260 BCE a body of Roman troops encamped nearby was attacked by Hamilcar and defeated with heavy loss. Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-punic-war">First Punic War</a> ended in 241 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> besieged and took Thermae, but treated it with unusual favour: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> reports that they allowed the Thermitani to govern their city and territory by their own laws as a reward for steady fidelity. In his time it was a flourishing place with considerable trade, and he praised its citizens' taste for art, noting that Scipio had restored statues to them after the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>.</p><p>Thermae seems to have become a colony in the time of Augustus. Few accounts survive of later periods, but its name appears in Ptolemy and the Itineraries, and the aqueduct and other remains suggest that it continued through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>. After the fall of the Roman Empire it declined, though it remained a bishopric until the 12th century.</p><p>From the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> to the early 19th century it was a major centre for the collection and shipping of grain, stored and taxed in a government warehouse complex, the caricatore regio. This made it one of the major ports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, with trade links to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>. The closing of the caricatore in the 19th century brought a deep economic crisis that lasted until the end of the 20th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Termini Imerese is a town of the Metropolitan City of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a>, about 33 km from Palermo. Its Roman remains include the largest and best preserved aqueduct bridge on the island and an amphitheatre probably of the Augustan age, built into houses. The Greek ruins of Himera lie within the comune's borders, and hot waters have flowed in the thermal baths in the old town since the Roman age.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Termini Imerese” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Survivors and Carthaginian settlers founded <b>Thermae Himeraeae</b> nearby in 407 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/himera">Himera</a></li></ul></section>
